Z||000009||WampusCat||08-03-2001||10:22 AM||||Happy birthday!  Yeah, I looked at 30 as a milestone too.  Kinda got spooked by the age creeping up on me and started doing 100 mile bike races and ran a marathon at 32.  I'm over that now and just do some light recreational jogging and mtn biking these days when I'm not playing with the car or sipping beer and admiring my tomato garden.  I turned 40 two months ago and it didn't seem like nearly such a big deal.  Just so you don't think it's all over at 30 though, check out this blurb from runnersworld.com:<P>"Aging faster: The age of the oldest marathoner to break three hours has nudged a bit closer to 70. Joe Henderson reports in his Running Commentary that Ed Whitlock, a British-born Canadian, ran 2:52:47 at the October 29 Columbus Marathon. Whitlock's age at the time was 69 years, 237 days, making him about six weeks older than British-American John Keston when he ran 2:58:33. Another former Brit living in the U.S., Clive Davies, ran 2:53:42 at a slightly younger 69 than Keston, while Keston remains the fastest over-70 runner with 3:00:58."||||reg||1
